  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDaily potential for valley fog will be the main theme as far as\nhazards are concerned. Fog will affect some aviation and \ndriving late at night through the early morning hours along \nriver valleys and deep hollers. Boundary layer winds will \nlikely weaken on a daily trend with decoupling at the surface to\nallow for clam surface flow. Meanwhile, relatively low or mid \ncloud free decks on a daily basis along with radiational \ncooling dropping temperatures to their dewpoints every night \nwill allow for the valley fog to form for much of the area. \n\nSome of the fog could become dense at certain locations, \nhowever most of the fog should be confined to the river \nvalleys where impacts may occur. Any fog should in fact \ndissipate by mid morning each day. Minor to medium impacts may \noccur for drivers and aviation if areas become susceptible to \ndense fog at times. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA nice high pressure blocking pattern will prevail into the \nshort term and even long term periods providing pleasant and \nquiet weather outside of some minor instances of potential\nshower or thunderstorm activity for a few days.\n\nA disturbance will have the potential to bring showers and/or \nthunderstorms to the area from Sunday evening through Monday \nafternoon. This will be confined to the southern portions of the\ncounty warning area but will likely make its way up into the \nsouthern coalfields and close to the Tri-state and Charleston \nMetro area. Therefore, we cannot rule out a few showers or storms\nduring peak heating hours elsewhere toward the southern half of\nthe area. This will include our southwestern VA counties as \nwell which will have the greatest probability of any \nconvection.\n\nAnother larger disturbance, a possible cut-off low pressure\nsystem coming in from the north, is forecast to setup just east\nof the area by midweek. This feature now does have chances of \nrain and/or thunderstorm potential than previous forecast \npackages which could potentially cause impacts under \nthunderstorm activity to the northeast mountains and along the \nmountains south of there on Tuesday and part of the first half \nof Wednesday. High pressure will then prevent the feature from \ntraversing into the area and should keep the area settled the \nrest of the work week.\n\nImpacts to the rest of the area will remain fairly quiet amid \nanother possible blocking high pressure system which will\npromote the lack of any rainfall chances for the rest of the\nwork week. Temperatures will still remain fairly seasonable and\npleasant for the short and long term forecast periods with only\nminimal impacts expected from fog or possible sub-severe \nthunderstorm activity.",
